---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const publishDate = '2026-08-30';
const title = '5 JSON Mistakes That Waste Everyone\'s Time';
const description = 'The recurring JSON mistakes that cause avoidable debugging sessions, and the quick fix for each.';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BlogPosting',
  headline: title,
  description,
  datePublished: publishDate,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'CodnexaHub' },
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 dark:text-slate-500 mb-1">August 30, 2026</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 font-medium mb-2">Blog</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p>
      None of these are exotic edge cases — they're the ordinary mistakes that show up in code
      review and bug reports every week, each one costing more debugging time than it should for
      how simple the actual fix is.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Trailing commas</h2>
    <p>
      JSON doesn't allow a trailing comma after the last item in an object or array — a habit picked
      up from JavaScript, where it's perfectly legal. This is one of the most common reasons a
      hand-edited JSON file suddenly fails to parse with an error that doesn't obviously point at
      the actual problem. Run it through the <a href="/tools/json-formatter">JSON Formatter</a>
      first; a parse error there is faster to diagnose than a cryptic failure deep in application
      code.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Assuming key order matters</h2>
    <p>
      JSON objects are unordered by specification, even though most parsers happen to preserve
      insertion order in practice. Code that depends on keys appearing in a specific order —
      including some naive diffing logic — is relying on undefined behavior that can break with a
      different parser or library version. If you need to compare two JSON payloads for meaningful
      differences, use a tool that understands this, like the <a href="/tools/json-diff">JSON Diff Checker</a>,
      rather than a plain text diff that treats reordered keys as changes.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Not escaping special characters in string values</h2>
    <p>
      A string value containing an unescaped quote, backslash, or literal newline is invalid JSON,
      not automatically escaped for you. This bites most often when building JSON by hand or via
      naive string concatenation instead of a proper serializer. The
      <a href="/tools/json-escape-tool">JSON String Escape Tool</a> handles this correctly, including
      the less obvious characters people forget.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Confusing null, missing, and empty</h2>
    <p>
      A key set to <code>null</code>, a key that's entirely absent, and a key set to an empty string
      or empty array are three different things, and treating them as interchangeable is a common
      source of subtle bugs — particularly with optional fields in API responses. Before writing
      code against an unfamiliar API's response shape, generate a proper schema with the
      <a href="/tools/json-schema-generator">JSON Schema Generator</a> and decide explicitly how each
      field's absence versus null versus empty should be handled, rather than discovering the
      distinction in production.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Building types by hand from a large nested response</h2>
    <p>
      Manually writing a TypeScript interface for a deeply nested API response is slow and
      introduces typos that only surface as confusing type errors later. If you're integrating with
      a new API, generate the starting types directly from a real sample response with
      <a href="/tools/json-to-typescript">JSON to TypeScript</a> rather than typing the whole
      structure from scratch.
    </p>

    <p>
      None of these require deep JSON expertise to avoid — they mostly require using a tool that
      catches the mistake before it becomes a debugging session. See the full
      <a href="/guides/json-essentials-guide">JSON Essentials guide</a> for a more complete walkthrough
      of formatting, validating, and converting JSON.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
